# egress-consent Slice 1 — fail-closed never-leak core + bug-report
consumer — apply ladder

Per `docs/plans/egress-consent-core-and-bug-report.md` and the owner-greenlit
design `loam-privacy-safe-data-sharing-design.md` (Slice 1 = the egress-consent
CORE §1 + the bug-report consumer §2; the health/analytics evaluator §3 is a
LATER slice, OUT OF SCOPE here).

Plan: `docs/plans/egress-consent-core-and-bug-report.md`.

Scope (plan §2 / §6 / §8): NEW component `framework/egress-consent/`. The
privacy floor that sits in front of EVERYTHING loam might send off the user's
machine, plus one consumer (the bug-report tool) that uses it.

The never-leak guarantee, enforced by construction (NOT a runtime promise):
the EgressBundle FSM defaults to a no-egress state and EVERY error path
(crash, timeout, abandoned review, ambiguous input) lands in a no-egress
state; the ONLY transition to RELEASED runs through a single, grep-audited
choke point — a deterministic, no-LLM, fail-closed release gate that refuses
(raising BEFORE any socket opens, mirroring the reversibility gate's
-32050-before-activate) on each of: not-approved, absent approval-binding,
binding-mismatch (the bundle mutated after approval — content-identity broke),
any still-pending item, unknown destination. The approval binds to the
content identity of the approved-or-redacted item set (structural_hash
semantics); any mutation invalidates it. The released payload contains EXACTLY
the approved + redacted items; declined items are STRUCTURALLY excluded; a
redacted item ships its replacement bytes, never the original.

Two-layer review surface (resolving the design's transparency-vs-simplicity
tension): a plain-language default view (numbered, abstraction-first voice,
ZERO internal vocabulary — reusing self-correction's find_internal_vocabulary
probe) + an always-available exact-bytes expansion that is byte-faithful to
what the gate would actually send (the label cannot lie about the contract).
Per-item approve / redact / decline.

The bug-report consumer: a `loam report` verb (registered on
loam.cli.subcommands, symmetric to `loam recover`) + a programmatic
entry-point. A short plain-language interview characterizes the issue, a
candidate bundle is assembled from LOCAL signals only (the user's note, loam
version, coarse OS — never username/paths), files and logs default to
DECLINED, and every candidate item's bytes are scanned against the
safety-layer secret floor (CONTENT_PATTERNS) and auto-redacted BEFORE the
user ever sees the review surface. The user then sends to a friendly intake
(no "GitHub" jargon shown) OR takes the always-available local-only fallback
(loam writes the report to the user's own disk + plain instructions, zero
egress). The fallback is first-class, never a dead end.

Lens 1 — almost entirely a wiring cycle over sealed primitives, consumed via
their public surfaces ONLY (zero sealed-component edits): the reversibility
ActivationGate's fail-closed posture, safety-layer's structural_hash +
CONTENT_PATTERNS secret floor, self-correction's vocab probe, the
loam.cli.subcommands group, and the output-to-disk convention. The only
genuinely-new code is the EgressBundle lifecycle + the release gate + the
review-surface controller + the bug-report assembly logic.

feedback_no_anthropic_api_key: the gate is DETERMINISTIC — no LLM inside the
gate, no network except the single allow-listed send at the very end of a PASS.

